    <section class="section">
      <h2>What is an Array?</h2>
      <p>
        An array is a linear data structure where elements are stored
        in contiguous memory locations. Arrays are used to store
        multiple values of similar type efficiently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Important Terms</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Element:</strong> Each value stored in the array</li>
        <li><strong>Index:</strong> Position of an element (starts from 0)</li>
        <li><strong>Size:</strong> Total number of elements in the array</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Why Use Arrays?</h2>
      <p>
        Arrays provide <strong>O(1)</strong> random access time.
        This means accessing any element using its index
        takes the same constant time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Array Syntax</h2>

      <p><strong>C / C++</strong></p>
      <div class="code-box">
        int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
      </div>

      <p><strong>Java</strong></p>
      <div class="code-box">
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Array Representation</h2>
      <p>
        Arrays are indexed from <strong>0</strong> to <strong>n-1</strong>.
        Each element can be accessed directly using its index value.
      </p>
    </section>

